{"h": {"measure": "A", "k": 100, "noise": "L"}, "sp": "nb", "rep": 128, "hyps": [{"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "Filling an empty direct input-output edge with a conv adds a missing path, whereas replacing an existing skip or pool on edge 3 removes a useful shortcut (e.g. #1-3, #10-11 vs #4-6)."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "Adding a residual shortcut where none existed gives the largest gains (#26 +25.8/+34.4/+15.1), more than replacing a conv or pool on edge 3."}, {"edit_edge": 0, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "Without the direct edge, removing edge 0 cuts the node-1 paths and leaves a shallow, sparse cell, which causes large drops (#23, #24)."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "With no direct path, removing edge 5 strips one of only two remaining routes into the output node (#18: -27.7 C10)."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "lower", "why": "If edge 4 is absent, edge 5 is the only route from intermediate nodes to the output, so deleting it collapses the cell's depth."}, {"edit_edge": 0, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "When the cell lacks a direct path, restoring edge 0 reconnects node 1 and its downstream paths, the inverse of the large losses from removing it."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "When edge 5 is empty, a conv on edge 3 adds needed capacity and an output path; in matched parent pairs it beats edge 5 filled on C10 (#2 vs #1, #5 vs #4, #7 vs #6), though not on every dataset."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "direction": "higher", "